irection, not evidence about demography. (ii) The UN WPP LOW demographic path biases its labour path DOWN, so its 2.95% is a LOWER bound conditional on its own closure — P2 says this, then uses the model's 2.95% only as something his 1.5% must be defended against, which is the wrong direction of inference. (iii) THE REGIONAL PROXY IS NOT THE QUESTION'S OBJECT AND ITS BIAS IS AMBIGUOUS, NOT NEUTRAL: the adapter proxies OECD-38 by us + oecd-ex-us, which EXCLUDES Japan, Korea and Turkiye (seasia/mena) and Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica (latam) while INCLUDING non-OECD states inside oecd-ex-us. Dropping Japan biases the proxy's growth UP; dropping Turkiye, Mexico and Korea biases it DOWN. P2's \"the adapter's own weights (0.629/0.371) agree to within 1 pp despite a different regional partition, so the weighting is not where the model and I diverge\" is exactly backwards as a reassurance: identical weights over non-identical country sets is where an unexamined composition error hides, not where it is ruled out. (iv) The model's China at 3.81%/yr — which P2 dismisses as \"simply does not implement the locked -2.7pp\" — is, under my first attack, approximately the RIGHT ORDER for an aging-inclusive China path, and its agreement with official medium-term projections is the one place the model corroborates rather than contradicts. P2 rejected the model's China number for the same reason his own China number is wrong. PREMORTEM, UPSIDE: it is Dec 31 2036 and coupled growth printed 3.1%/yr, far above the Wave-1 centre of 1.68%. What happened: the locked healthy-aging capacity gain (\"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\", 41 countries, N ~ 1M, +0.4 pp/yr to global growth) was treated by every Wave-1 seat as a rounding term inside an hours calculation (+0.25%/yr participation in P2) rather than as the growth contribution 2A actually states — and it arrived as a participation surge in the 60-74 band once statutory retirement ages extended with life expectancy, because the binding constraint on older-worker participation was never capability, it was pension-accrual cliffs, which fiscal strain forced countries to flatten. The labour-scarcity demand pull that ALL THREE Wave-1 seats conceded (every one of them moved AI-Q2 up) showed up in measured TFP after all, because the 2015-22 TFP baseline they all used as the counterfactual was itself depressed by the slack labour market that aging then removed. China did not get the -2.7 pp subtracted twice. Tariff-walled OECD manufacturing investment and the grid build raised measured capital deepening above the +0.35 pp everyone assumed. Coupled growth cleared 2.6% and CQ6-A resolved. PREMORTEM, DOWNSIDE: it printed 0.9%/yr, below every Wave-1 P10 but P1's. What happened: the AI-Q4 impairment landed in 2029-31 and the digestion ran through the entire resolution window; the grid interconnection and transformer queues that were supposed to be the bottleneck turned out to be the CAUSE, because the electricity needed for the buildout was never built and the write-down took the capital stock with it; China's pension-subsidy claim went from 10.1% of fiscal expenditure to a hard financing event; the eroding sovereign convenience yield from 2A became a funding cost that killed the long-duration capital formation on both legs at once; and the BIS Box C demand shortfall was realized exactly as written — displaced cognitive labour income never recovered, the care transfer was refused, and the rationed care hours showed up as forgone output in the measured aggregate. P10 1.0 / P90 3.3 is deliberately WIDER than every Wave-1 interval on the upside because the above-tail is the one all three structurally suppressed.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.940Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"An official medium-term China real GDP projection (IMF WEO or Worl